 ## Overview
-This method defines a custom 16-bit floating point format to represent the frequency domain spectrum of volumetric data, reducing memory cost compared to 64-bit double precision while maintaining acceptable precision for volume rendering. It is specifically designed for frequency domain volume rendering, where the wide dynamic range of spectral coefficients necessitates floating point representation over integer formats. It differs from spatial domain compression methods by operating in the frequency domain and is distinct from sibling hardware-specific compressions by being a custom format tailored for spectral data rather than general spatial voxels.
+This method defines a custom 16-bit floating point data type used to store the frequency domain spectrum of a volume during frequency-domain volume rendering [S1, S2, unofficial]. It solves the problem of excessive memory consumption associated with standard 64-bit double precision representations of spectral data, while maintaining sufficient numerical precision to avoid visible artifacts in the final rendered image [S1, S2, unofficial]. It is distinct from sibling methods like [Color Packing (RGBA)](5142-color-packing-rgba.md) which target spatial domain voxel storage, and [ELSCINT1/PMSCT_RLE1 Delta Decoding](11897-elscint1-pmsct-rle1-delta-decoding.md) which targets spatial domain compression via run-length encoding [S1, S2, unofficial].
 
 ## How it works
 **Inputs and Outputs:**
-- Input: Volumetric data (typically real-valued, e.g., CT scans) transformed into the frequency domain (via Hartley or Fourier transform).
-- Output: A compressed spectral representation using 16-bit floating point values, ready for inverse transformation or direct frequency domain rendering.
+*   **Input:** A complex-valued frequency domain spectrum (or real-valued Hartley spectrum) derived from a spatial volume.
+*   **Output:** A memory buffer containing the spectrum values packed into the custom 16-bit format.
 
